Sval: the URL classifier steps aside for the features the content classifier replaces

A backport of mozilla-central's
privacy.trackingprotection.urlclassifier.disable_for_channel_classifier pref
and its check in AntiTrackingChannelClassifierUtils::CheckChannelHelper,
narrowed: with it on, the URL classifier's tracking features stay off, so
the two engines never match the same tracking lists, while the
consent-manager and anti-fraud annotations and the harmful add-on protection
keep running, as in Firefox. Safe Browsing does not run through this path.

The URL classifier's features complete before the content classifier
decides, so a channel they stopped is left alone and the content classifier
sees their annotations, and it skips a channel
UrlClassifierCommon::ShouldProcessWithProtectionFeature says protection
skips, as Firefox's own protection features do.

The check sits inside a C++ function; no chrome API can skip it.

